\section{Equivalence}{

Equivalence is defined by stbl's own conversions:
\itemize{
\item Values of the same type are equivalent only if they are \code{\link[=identical]{identical()}}.
\item Values of different types are equivalent when \code{identical(to(x, y), y)} and
\code{identical(to(y, x), x)}. A failed conversion means "not equivalent".
}

Requiring both directions keeps lossy conversions out. For example,
\code{to_lgl(0.5)} is \code{TRUE}, but \code{to_dbl(TRUE)} is \code{1}, so \code{0.5} and \code{TRUE} are
not equivalent. Neither are \code{"1.0"} and \code{1L}, nor \code{"true"} and \code{TRUE}.

Equivalent pairs include \code{1L} and \code{1}, \code{1L} and \code{TRUE}, \code{"1"} and \code{1L},
\code{"TRUE"} and \code{TRUE}, \code{as.Date("2024-01-02")} and \code{"2024-01-02"}, and a
factor and the character vector of its labels. A factor and an ordered
factor are not equivalent, and neither are two factors with the same labels
but different levels, because those pairs have the same type and are not
identical. The same rule makes date-times with different time zones not
equivalent. \code{NA} matches only \code{NA}, and \code{NaN} matches only \code{NaN}.

Conversions between Date and POSIXct values use the time zone of the
date-time value, so results don't depend on the time zone of your session
(unless the date-time has no time zone of its own).

Equivalence is not strictly transitive: \code{"TRUE"} is equivalent to \code{TRUE}, and
\code{TRUE} to \code{1L}, but \code{"TRUE"} is not equivalent to \code{1L}. When \code{...} contains
three or more values, every pair must be equivalent.
}

\section{Narrowest type}{

When values are equivalent, the result has the narrowest type of the inputs,
by this fixed ranking (narrowest first): factor, Date, POSIXct, hms, Period,
logical, integer, double, character. Classed types rank ahead of their
underlying types, so a result keeps the class a caller asked for.

Values of a type outside the ranking (for example, classes handled by the
\code{\link[vctrs:vec_cast]{vctrs::vec_cast()}} fallback in \code{\link[=to]{to()}}) are equivalent only to values of the
same class, compared with \code{\link[vctrs:vec_equal]{vctrs::vec_equal()}}.
}

\section{Containers}{

Lists, data frames, \code{NULL}, and functions are separate categories, and
values of different categories are never equivalent. For example, \code{1:2} and
\code{list(1, 2)} are not equivalent, even though \code{\link[=to_lst]{to_lst()}} and \code{\link[=to_int]{to_int()}}
convert between them.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Lists} are reconciled element by element, with the same rules applied
to every element. Length, names, and other attributes must match.
\item \strong{Data frames} are reconciled column by column. The class, row names, and
column names and order must match.
\item \strong{Functions} and \code{NULL} are equivalent only when identical.
}

The result can combine the narrowest elements of its inputs, so it might not
equal any single input. For \code{list(a = 1L, b = 2)} and \code{list(a = 1, b = 2L)},
the result is \code{list(a = 1L, b = 2L)}.
}

